     {
      "text": "اِسْتَيْقَظَ مُبَكِّرًا",
      "tr": "to wake up early"
     },
     {
      "text": "اِسْتَيْقَظَ مِنَ النَّوْمِ",
      "tr": "to wake up from sleep"
     }
    ],
    "phrases": [],
    "synonyms": [
     {
      "word": "صَحَا",
      "register": "neutral",
      "nuance": "to wake up, to come to — shorter; the dialects use it every day (صِحِي)"
     },
     {
      "word": "قَامَ مِنَ النَّوْمِ",
      "register": "colloquial",
      "nuance": "to get up from sleep — waking and getting up in one"
     }
    ],
    "antonyms": [
     {
      "word": "نَامَ",
      "note": "to sleep, to fall asleep"
     }
    ],
    "etymology": {
     "text": "Form X (اِسْتَفْعَلَ) of the root ي ق ظ “to be awake”; Wiktionary: to wake up (intransitive), to wake someone up (transitive), to pay attention, to become aware of. The same root gives يَقِظ “alert, awake”.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "The root ي ق ظ is about being alert: يَقَظَة is wakefulness or vigilance, and يَقِظ an alert, watchful person."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "Form X: the past starts with اِسْتَـ (اِسْتَيْقَظْتُ), the present with يَسْتَـ and has i before the last root letter: يَسْتَيْقِظُ. The first alif is a connecting hamza: silent inside a sentence.",
     "To wake someone else up is أَيْقَظَ (form IV): أَيْقَظَتْنِي أُمِّي “my mother woke me up”."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "Waking up and getting up are two verbs: اِسْتَيْقَظْتُ ثُمَّ نَهَضْتُ “I woke up, then I got up”."
    ]
   }
