     "غُرْفَةُ النَّوْمِ, the bedroom, is “the room of sleep” — the verbal noun of نَامَ."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "A hollow verb with i in the short stem: نِمْتُ “I slept” (not *نُمْتُ, which is the pattern of قُلْتُ); present يَنَامُ with ā.",
     "The long ā shortens before a consonant ending and in the jussive: نِمْنَا (we slept), لَمْ يَنَمْ (he didn’t sleep), نَمْ! (sleep!)."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "One verb covers “sleep”, “fall asleep” and “go to bed”; to stress the moment of falling asleep Arabic also says غَلَبَهُ النَّوْمُ “sleep overcame him”."
    ]
